The (vanilla) game was considered inferior to Dks1 by a lot of players because:

-  the lack of world interconnectivity, and worse level design;

- the nonsensical geography and transitions from areas to areas;

- it lacks really memorable boss battles, and has too much bosses overall, they also were considered repetitive (big guy with a sword);

- the game didn't look as good as the previews we saw, the lightning system they had was basically taken out from the game (probably to run on Ps3/Xbox360);

- gameplay is different from the other games, and people had a hard time to adapt;

- things like adaptability (a stat that increases roll invincibility frames) and Soul Memory (the way matchmaking works) were perceived as bad and unnecessary changes.

Basically, vanilla version was a good game, but far from the incredible game that Dks1 was for a lot of people.

Now though, we got three dlcs, patches and the Scholar of the First Sin edition, which changes the game a lot.



The three DLCs, are the best part of Dks2, and they do have the great level design Dks1 had. They also have cool gameplay mechanics, bosses, and a lot of armors, rings and weapons.

The Scholar edition is the best version available, it has all the dlcs, changes to the enemy placement, tons of new details that brings some life and secrets to areas.

Items dropped by hackers can only cause issues if :

- they are upgraded more than they should be (a +10 boss weapon doesn't exist, only +5 max);

- they are +14, this is impossible and should crash your game, there is a way to remove it using [cheat software];

- they are infused when they can't be, like poison binoculars or simply weapons infused with stuff that shouldn't be possible.
